From 5a07baaf667257473e2f01f4f3e8ca0a825fb5f3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 04:39:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix pointer compared with integer constant 0 folding as not
 equal

In C a pointer may be compared with the integer constant 0. The code
generator kept the 0 as an integer constant (IM_NONE) while the pointer
operand of the comparison was a pointer constant (IM_ABSOLUTE 0), so
the constant folding of pointer comparisons, which compares the memory
class, folded p == 0 to false and p != 0 to true. This showed with -O2,
-Os and -O3 for a file scope or static pointer initialised to 0 that is
never written, because the global optimizer substitutes the initializer,
and at every level for (char *)0 == 0. With the 0 on the left the
comparison took the integer path instead, which compares only the
offset, so 0 == &x folded to true.

Emit the 0 as a null pointer constant, the same constant the pointer to
bool conversion uses, so both operands of the comparison are pointers,
and take the comparison type from the pointer operand. The zero that the
logical not of a pointer compares against had the same defect when the
result is used as a value, so mark it as a null pointer constant too.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01J5rsu7nnbQYnRY7GP34gdn
---
 autotest/autotest.bat          |   3 +
 autotest/nullptrcmptest.c      | 132 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 oscar64/InterCodeGenerator.cpp |  28 +++++++
 3 files changed, 163 insertions(+)
